A||||40||Spektyr||Fiero Handling Characteristics||||10||
Z||000000||Spektyr||04-22-2001||06:26 PM||||Okay, I got a basically empty parking lot to myself last night, so I grabbed my dual black crayons and scribbled all over it.  (Incidentally I had the un-official permission of the building security guard.)<P>First of all, I had a BLAST!  All stock my 85 GT's V6 would burn the tires pretty much as long as I wanted to.<P>However... it very quickly became apparent to me that the Fiero handles significantly different than a front engine car.  I've read all kinds of stuff about the suspension, weight transfer, etc, etc on these cars and cars like them.  I just can't seem to find any seat-of-the-pants stuff.<P>I didn't have enough room to really throw the car around, I had to keep it relatively tame (for safety concerns like lightpoles, and the ever-present law problems).  As the back end of the car began to swing wide it felt a lot different than a front engine, and it acted like it was going to do something MUCH different if I kept pushing it.  Well, without more room and more safety from cops, I didn't push it to find out what it wanted to do.<P>Can anyone put into words what it is that Fieros do differently?  What they do better/worse?  One major thing I'm looking for is the "Don't ever to THIS, you'll wipe out and not recover till you stop."<P>Like doing power fishtails on a dirt road in a frontwheel drive by jerking the wheel from side to side... odds are you'll fishtail too far, suddenly overcorrect and spin into the ditch, fence, whatever.  My buddy vaulted his Honda Civic doing that.  (And lucky me was in the passenger seat for the ride...)<P>Don't get me wrong, I'm not looking for "How to Hotrod your Fiero on the Street".  I'm more interested in what they can and can't do, and how the car 'tells' you when it's about to do something you won't like.<P>There simply aren't a lot of options in my area for people who want to really run a car hard to find out what it's limits are so they don't ever exceed them on the street where it would be dangerous to others.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||10
Z||000001||fldevel||04-22-2001||06:47 PM||||the major thing ive noticed is when going into a power spin .. say at like 40 mph r so the ass end tends to swing around and stay there .. next to impossible to get the front end to swing back around .. also alot of fun to do dounuts ..i had the privilage to get a whole church parking lot to myself large enough to get up to about 45 mph and still have enough time to stop before i got to the other end  ..was having a lil too much fun that day .. as i recall i ended up having to replace my motor mounts ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wade87GT||04-22-2001||06:49 PM||||How about joining up with your local SCCA. Those people have all kinds of info. They probably have a track that you run your car on too. Ours uses an abandoned part of Pensacola Naval Air Station (small runway and taxiway). As I'm sure you've already found out, you learn alot by practicing.<P>Wade||||reg||14
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||04-22-2001||07:07 PM||||Fiero's suffer from trailing throttle oversteer.. Go into a corner and lift off the rear end comes around. Driving a fiero fast on an autocross course takes a lot of skill , I have been running my car for 5 years now in Canada and the US.<P>The car will go from terminal understeer to oversteer very quickly , there are various ways to get rid of this condition. I found driving the Fiero to be much like a 911 , both have a tendancy to come around in a corner during a drop throttle condition. I have been autocrossing and tracking a fiero , 87 vette and most recently a 911 RSR. The vette is the easiest to drive , point and shoot , power steering gobs of torque to steer the rear with. The fiero requires all of your attention where as the vette and 911 are far more forgiving in sticky situations<P>Just my view on things ..after 5 years of autocrossing the fiero I finally got the car to the point where it is no longer a chore driving it fast on a track , it exhibits none of the typical fiero traits (understeer) ...but it took a while<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektyr||04-22-2001||07:22 PM||||So basically if you go into the corner too hot, you understeer and miss the line.  If you get off the throttle, you oversteer and miss the line.<P>Yup... definitely not a forgiving setup.  Better not even THINK about the brakes...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ed||04-22-2001||09:02 PM||||I've only added a rear sway bar, but that helped negate the understeer that GM built in (remember the Corvair!).  <P>Having said that, I'd support Wade87GT in saying that you should try out your car on a safe track.  I went to a "street performance driving school" sponsored by the Sports Car Club of America (SCCA). That is a good place to get accustomed to your car, and learn how to avoid the dreaded spin. Incidentally, I saw several cars go spinning off course, much to the chagrin of the safety guy that day! I never came close to going off.  The instructor(s) even pointed out I had a lot of handling left that I could use.  That told me that the car is "better" than I am, and I probably drive conservatively enough to keep myself out of trouble.      <P>Drop throttle oversteer is a real threat, agreeing with slammedFiero, so don't put yourself in a situation where you have to back off or hit the brakes in the corner or you will/may spin. Power slides forever!<P>Having said all that, I've found driving my GT for the last ten years has been a hoot!  And it handles as well or better than most cars, and I've had other performance cars. If you want excitement, punch an '64 Pontiac GTO in the rain.  And brakes?  Forgetaboutit. <P>P.S. For controlled track conditions only: Downshift, brake BEFORE THE CORNER, hit the power and go through. Go in slow and out fast.  No spins.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tryxalon||04-22-2001||09:16 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B> ... I found driving the Fiero to be much like a 911 ... The fiero requires all of your attention where as the vette and 911 are far more forgiving in sticky situations JM</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh, that is definitely true!!<BR>I also agree with the Porsche 911 comparison!! <P>But that "idiosyncrasy" is also the joy of the car!  A Fiero REQUIRES that you actually DRIVE the car ... kinda like a 4 wheel motorcycle.   You have to brake or downshift <B> before </B> the curve and then power out - <P>Ah!! Nothing like a "sports car" to make you feel alive!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Most unlike the totally isolated "autopilot" barges such as a Lincoln or a big Caddy, a Fiero forces you to drive it!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>By the way: I run P195 60R14's in front and P215 60R14's in the rear with the factory WS6 suspension and that keeps the normal understeer to a lower level. I've got aftermarket rubber cradle bushings and I don't find "deceleration oversteer" to be a large problem - but then, I'm <I> driving the car </I> and not just "along for the ride". Forethought keeps such situations rare.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 04-22-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000007||mrossum||04-22-2001||11:57 PM|||| sorry, duplicate post<p>[This message has been edited by mrossum (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrossum||04-23-2001||12:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Just my view on things ..after 5 years of autocrossing the fiero I finally got the car to the point where it is no longer a chore driving it fast on a track , it exhibits none of the typical fiero traits (understeer) ...but it took a while<P>JM</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am interested in how you set up your car, slammed fiero. I run mine in SCCA FSP, but have been having a horrible time chasing the set-up. Any help would be appreciated. <P>Charley J||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||04-23-2001||12:17 AM||||Geez ..where to start...<P>-Polyurathane everywhere.<BR>-poly cradle bushings , thinking of solid   mount<BR>-ultimate dog bone<BR>Koni's up front , one click from full soft,<BR>carrera race weight springs ( I don't remember the Lbs rating off hand), moog problem solver ball joints<BR>Custom made sway bars front and rear<BR>KOni's out back , full hard , Ground control coil overs with new 375lbs eibachs for this season (haven't tried it yet , used to run 325lbs)<P>I don't know my alignment off hand , I do know one of the biggest differences I have felt recently is when I switched to 255/40/17's rear , and 225/45/17's front , pirelli P zero C's. These are what come standard on a C2 911 , I took precident from 911's , Big tire out back , the car is very predictable , no understeer at an autocross and you can drift the car easily on the track and feel very comfortable (even a 23year old kid like me) <P>I have been through lots and lots of setups , big bars in the rear , small front , 3 different sets of springs. For what I use the car for this is the best so far , i do want to get a tubular front end when money permits (held motorsports sport front end) This isn't a big dollar set up but a well researched one , I have tried lots of combos<P>I suggest for indfo on autocrossing or racing / improving your fiero's handling in general , join the Fiero racing list , I'm the moderator of it , 160 people , this is what we talk about all the time .. this is how I developed my car..<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eric||04-23-2001||01:15 AM||||Fieros have most of their weight focused toward the center axis of the horizontal plane.  When you drive a front engine car, like a Corvette, or a rear engine car, like a Porsche, you're driving a car with most of  its weight at the poles.  In a situation where oversteer is induced, these cars will step out of line slower, and seemingly more controlable.  A Fiero will snap out of line while a Porsche will just slowly start to drift.  <P>To remedy this dangerous problem, use ballasts like bricks or sandbags in the front and back compartments.||||reg||1
Z||000011||pontiacs4me||04-23-2001||02:26 AM||||the first time I drove my car was in the last week of febuary.  We had snow on the ground and ice on the parking lots.  I was driving to a friend of mines house to show him the car for the first time, and when I pulled into the parking lot of the apartments where he lives I got a little sideways.  I corrected the fishtail and let off the gas.  When this would happen in my firebird it would straighten itself out then I would continue on normally.  When I corrected the rear of the car swung further out to the other side, so I corrected again, this time the rear swung all the way around, and I came to a stop facing the way I was coming from.  I also had bald tires on the back, so this may of been another factor to the spinning out.||||reg||1
Z||000012||pontiacs4me||04-23-2001||02:26 AM||||oh yeah, and it was a big parking lot, and I luckily didnt hit anything or anybody.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cameron||04-23-2001||02:28 AM||||The thing I like about the fiero is that it is balanced such that in a powerslid or spin it is very perdictible...I have noticed during a powerslide I have a much easier time hitting my mark on the exit of it.  Where with a front engine car the rear end tends to try and get infront of the engine.  The Fiero is quite content to have the engine and the front end right next to each other going sideways.  Anybody ever try to powerslide into a parking spot.  Come up 90 degrees off the parking spot.  Turn the wheel, step on the gas, break the rear end so that you are facing the parking spot, counterstear to straighten it out and push in the clutch or foot off the gas, the wheels catch and push you into the parking spot.  I recommend that you don't do it when there are cars on either side of the spot; it can become quite dangerous.  I have seen it done in a 2000 Mustang GT, but it takes much more work then doing it in the fiero.<P>( P.S. Don't try this at home  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Vlad||04-23-2001||04:41 AM||||Guys,<P>How do I join Fiero racing list?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000015||Lore||04-23-2001||04:58 AM||||The handling characteristics I like the most in the Fiero are that the tendencies to understeer and oversteer are pretty much the same.  Let me provide an example.  A friend of mine used to have a 94 Mustang Cobra with a 5 speed.  He let me drive it once and I took it into a corner, downshifted into second, and dropped the cluth so that I could see its reaction.  The rear end FLEW out from underneath me and it oversteered like crazy.  I expected this somewhat, just not as much.  I managed to gain control of it while the person sitting at the stop light just stared at me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  When I do this with my Fiero, instead of it oversteering, the rear end AND the front end both slide into the corner and I end up getting the car sliding on all 4 ends.  That way I can keep it pretty straight though the corner because both ends are sliding at the same velocity.<P>Lore      ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Will||04-23-2001||11:02 AM||||You must have a hell of a Fiero if it has 4 ends! Mine just has two. Was that a rare factory option?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I took my '87 GT to my first AutoX yesterday. It was a blast.<BR>I have a WS6 front swaybar on the rear, rod end sway bar links all around, 235/60-15 fr and 245/60-15 rr Dunlop GT Qualifiers (I know they're cheap, but they fit my budget)... Oh yeah, and worn out shocks at all four corners.<P>Anyway, my car oversteers, but the great thing is that, exactly opposite a front engine car, power pulls you <I>out</I> of oversteer. IOW, with my car I can come into a corner hot, trail brake pretty hard and get the rear end coming out, then roll into the throttle at the apex to bring the car back into line for a fast exit.<P>There's a saying: "understeer scares the driver, oversteer scares the passenger".<BR>I'd rather have an oversteering setup than an understeering one; oversteer is driver correctable, understeer isn't.<P>Look up your local SCCA chapter and start going to AutoX's. And talk to the guys who get the best times. That's where you'll really learn.<P>The Fiero racing list can be found on <A HREF="http://www.groups.yahoo.com" TARGET=_blank>www.groups.yahoo.com</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Slammed Fiero||04-23-2001||11:51 AM||||The 84 to 87 Fiero is not a well balanced car , It can be made a well balanced car , but out of the box it is far from it. 88's are much closer to a balanced predictible car. Best handling Fiero I have driven? an 88 coupe with a quad 4 and some basic street suspension mods , bars , coil overs koni's. While steady state cornering was no better than my car , the ability to toss the car like a 914 or Mk1 Mr2 was amazing in comparison to a GT with a boat anchor 2.8 (sorry there heavy), You didn't have to pay as much attention , car weighed in at 2375lbs , as to my GT's 2794Lbs curb weight. The car allowed for a much less focused driver on the track ..ie you could have fun.. I have spun my Fiero at 75Mph on the track and it is not an experience I want to repeat.  (old suspension set up)<P><BR>Well balanced according to many books on car handling is not trailing throttle oversteer and gobs of understeer. Will is quite right on the undesteer scaring the driver issue. 99% of Fiero drivers will go out an off ramp pushing , they will get to the point of massive understeer , loose there line in the corner and back off.. The minute you back off around the car will go. I have done it many times. I'm suprised with your set up will that you get oversteer. Does the car go directly into oversteer or does it exhibt some understeer traits before the transition. My first year autocrossing , I had a bone stock setup , the car is low mile (18,000) , I had new GM shocks , but that was it, the car would go from rampid understeer to terminal oversteer before you could make a correction you were spun around. This has to be the most frustrating thing a driver/autocrosser/racer can experience , there is no correction , the mass out back and stock sized tires makes the car hard to catch and correct when it starts swapping ends. The 84 to 87 Fiero is a lot like a earlier 911(back to the 911 again) Takes a lot of skill to drive , the later 911's such as C2's exhibit some of the same trailing throttle oversteer but to a much lesser extent , like an 88 Fiero.  Like it has been said before , do all your braking and what not before a corner , use the Torque to your advantage , to puull you out of the corner. I know certian set ups for fiero's that you can go in a little hotter , lift off and the rear end will come around (again 911 porsche but to a lesser extent) , a bit of a sloppy driving style , but nothing puts a bigger smile on my face than oversteer.<P>Don't get me wrong I love my Fiero and I come from a huge Mid / Rear engined family , 2 911's , 3 Corvairs and a Fiero. Fiero's take a lot of research to get to the point where they are truly a well balanced car..ie neutral. Took me 5 years of bugging the hell out of anyone who had seat time in a Fiero. <BR>Drive your Fiero for fun , Don't dump thousands into the suspension to make it a class winner at an autocross..drive it for enjoyment. <P>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||04-23-2001||11:52 AM||||If you go into a turn 'too fast' in any car you are going to end up in the ditch.<P> You aways want to brake before the curve, and then once you are in it power back up and keep pushing it till the rear just starts to break loose. <P>The best way to get practice at this is in a parking lot with a few inches of snow, or in a downpour.<P>I would like to know where you guys are geting your free tires from? Im not so willing to leave mine smeared all over a parking lot when a good set of treads will set you back around 4 bills.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Spektyr||04-23-2001||01:54 PM||||Buy some decent treaded used tires.  The cheapest ones you can find that have at least matching pairs, and buy some cheapo wheels to mount them on.<P>No, it won't tell you exactly how the car handles on good rubber, but you can have some fun and learn the basics.  Once you bald the tires, throw them away and get another set the next time you want to scrawl black all over a parking lot.<P>Not to mention you have four decent-sized spares in the garage that way...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000020||ChadMan||04-23-2001||02:54 PM||||I had my first autocross yesterday too...got my butt kicked by a bunch of Miatas and MR2's.<P>My biggest problem was understeer.  I couldn't power out of any corner without the nose drifting wide.  I'm sure part of the problem is that my new Firestones up front aren't as sticky as my old Yokos out back, but I would welcome any advice on how to tame this nasty "poosh" (NASCAR terminology).||||reg||1
Z||000021||Cameron||04-23-2001||06:03 PM||||ok just fyi, I do agree that they aren't perfectly balanced, but from my position I come from front engined mustle cars, mostly Mustangs, Corvettes (older ones, before they figured out sports car, except the z06 can only do 1.0 lateral gs), Jaguars, etc.  And those cars are ok when under alittle power but if you decelerate or rapidly accelerate they slip and slide everywhere.  Where the Fiero yes may have under/over steer problems but you can get around them by driving more proactively.  Admitedly, you can't just jump into a Fiero and start driving it like a pro, you have to have some experience behind you.  Where the Mustangs and Corvettes you can just jump into and drive.||||reg||1
Z||000022||ray b||04-23-2001||06:36 PM||||free driver school without pain is G P L grand prix legends by papyrus,sierra free demo down load at ["Welcome to The Pits"]site<BR>or try ["pappy"]site or search for gpl 100's of sites on net.<BR> Is not a game it is a simulation of 1967<BR>gp F-1 cars mid eng pre-wings. as trainer they have F-2 and f-3 cars allso 67 that are full adjustable springs, shocks, roll bars, ride higth, bump rubbers, tire press, ect.<BR>the sim is real feel of race cars and best model of under/over steer with trail throttle<BR>snap with out fear of death.<BR>gpl is best with a wheel+petals but can be use in keyboard or joystick modes.<BR>EVERY FIERO DRIVER SHOULD TRY GPL||||reg||1
Z||000023||Wade87GT||04-23-2001||09:21 PM||||For years I drove (hard) my 87 GT with a mostly stock suspension. I did have a bigger front and rear swaybar kit from the Fiero Store, an Ultimate Dogbone, and 225-50-16 GoodYear Eagle GT II all around. The car was so balanced that I could produce a 4-wheel drift almost at will. After playing a bit too hard, I wore out everything that was made from rubber. Then I did the whole RCC thing (except bump-steer kit) and poly mounts everywhere else. I still trying to dial it all in. Right now there is either huge, "that pole's coming our way" understeer or spleem ripping side load g-forces. I'm persistant though.<P>Wade||||reg||14
Z||000024||KRMFiero||04-23-2001||09:32 PM||||one thing i noticed when i took mine to a parking lot, the front plows alor when turning hard going 10+ mph||||reg||1
Z||000025||Wannabe||04-23-2001||11:01 PM||||So what is well balanced? Try a powerful front driver...you can trai brake well into the corner and get good grip, but as you try to add power on the exit, the tires spin like crazy. My AWD Talon would plow into a turn if you went in a bit to hot, and if you tried to lift even a bit, the car would snap into oversteer as fast as any Fiero. Every car has it's own particular handling caracteristics. Ultimately, it comes down to what you enjoy driving and which handling traits you are most comfortable with. ||||reg||1
Z||000026||gixxer||04-23-2001||11:02 PM||||My '88 handles fine. No bad traits in particular.<BR>A bit of power oversteer in the lower gears.<BR>Bit of oversteer on trailing throttle(put the power back on, moron!).<BR>It just takes a bit of nerve and confidence in your ability, and the cars'.<BR>You have to feel what it's doing (and anticipate what it's gonna do) thru your hands and your butt. If you can't process that sort of input in your head, you should be driving a Camry.<BR>If you lower it onto its' bumpstops, have no-name tires and crummy shocks, you deserve what you get! ( <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000027||gixxer||04-23-2001||11:06 PM||||That's supposed to be a halo around my face, as I am holier-than-thou.<BR>Heh! ||||reg||10
Z||000028||Cameron||04-23-2001||11:34 PM||||Only problem I have with my fiero is 1) my clutch...it sucks...keeps slipping on me.  2) I want a bit more power.  But I just got through with a drive.  But I can definately get it to do a very graseful 4 wheel drift.  Loads of fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||Posthumane||04-23-2001||11:49 PM||||Ray, i agree. GPL is definately the best driving sim you will find. That is where i learned to handle lift oversteer, drifting, etc. before i started pushing any real cars. (need wheel and pedals for it). I haven't pushed my fiero yet as i purchased it quite recently and havent gotten everything fully operational yet, but i will start autoXing soon to test it.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Will||04-23-2001||11:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>... a good set of treads will set you back around 4 bills.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I guess that's why mine suck. I only paid three bills for them.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR>free driver school without pain is G P L grand prix legends by papyrus,sierra free demo down load at ["Welcome to The Pits"]site or try ["pappy"]site or search for gpl 100's of sites on net.<BR>Is not a game it is a simulation of 1967 gp F-1 cars mid eng pre-wings. as trainer they have F-2 and f-3 cars allso 67 that are full adjustable springs, shocks, roll bars, ride higth, bump rubbers, tire press, ect.<BR>the sim is real feel of race cars and best model of under/over steer with trail throttle<BR>snap with out fear of death.<BR>gpl is best with a wheel+petals but can be use in keyboard or joystick modes.<BR>EVERY FIERO DRIVER SHOULD TRY GPL<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree. I drive the HELL out of my car, but am terrible at racing video games. Why? No input on the ass-o-meter.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Will||04-24-2001||12:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>, car weighed in at 2375lbs , as to my GT's 2794Lbs curb weight. The car allowed for a much less focused driver on the track ..</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What else was done to the car? That 420 lbs could NOT have all come from the engine... unless a Q4 weighs about 50 lbs.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Cameron||04-24-2001||03:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> I disagree. I drive the HELL out of my car, but am terrible at racing video games. Why? No input on the ass-o-meter.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You know that could be any more accurate.  I don't know about everybody else but when I was taught to drive and shift it comes from the feeling you get through your butt.  Tachometers take too much time too look down and see, the vibrations through the pedals are bent, the only place to get an accurate reading is though your shoulder blades and your butt.  Maybe this is taking your statement a little to seriously, but it seems the be the best for me while driving.  And yes I do catch myself looking down at the tach just to get my bearings sometimes, but only because it's habit.  Should have learned race driving right out of the hole, but oh well, what doesn't kill you makes you stronger.<P><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||ChadMan||04-24-2001||08:49 AM||||Hey, does Grand Prix Legends support force-feedback?  I have a Logitech Wingman Formula Force wheel, if it matters.||||reg||1
Z||000034||elfiero||04-24-2001||11:13 AM||||IMHO,until you have the car in a full four-<BR>wheel drift,you have not reached the ultimate<BR>cornering limit of the tires/suspension.<BR>my backround;25 years of driving racecars of<BR>all types,sizes,horsepower.<BR>P.S.at this point,if you have a passenger,<BR>he/she is screaming and/or piddling in there<BR>pants!||||reg||6
Z||000035||Spektyr||04-24-2001||12:55 PM||||Note to self:  include plastic passenger seat cover in suspension upgrade plans.<P>And I totally agree with the driver intensive Fiero vs. autopilot Lincoln reference made above.  I own one of each (see signature line) and a lobotomized lab monkey could drive the Lincoln because, well, it just WON'T perform.  At least not stock.  In cornering you have 3 options.  Take it easy like your grandma, go in fast plow straight ahead and jump the curb, or go in fast, nail the brake or gas hard and spin it sideways.  Heck, it might just roll over out of spite.  (certainly feels like it with the body roll it does).<P>My Lincoln is for days when I want to be invincible (heck, if you did lose control, not much out there can total a Lincoln), when I want to feel like I'm sitting on the sofa at home watching a movie, or when I want to carry more than one person.  It will also be my winter driver as the Fiero is squirrelly enough in the rain.  The final "Lincoln" use I forsee is when I want to go get parts for the Fiero that don't fit nicely into the tiny trunk.  The Town car has a 2.5 dead body trunk in it.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||Taijiguy||04-24-2001||02:18 PM||||All this talk of letting off the throttle in the middle of a slide...what are you all, a bunch of wussies?? That's not when you get <B>off</B> the throttle, that's when you HAMMER the sucker!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000037||Will||04-24-2001||02:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B>All this talk of letting off the throttle in the middle of a slide...what are you all, a bunch of wussies?? That's not when you get [b]off</B> the throttle, that's when you HAMMER the sucker!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah! Getting on the throttle will bring the front end around and get you out of the slide. Of course you may not be pointed the direction you want, but do you really expect to have your cake and eat it too?||||reg||1
Z||000038||Spektyr||04-24-2001||03:02 PM||||Hehe, it's not like people are putting V8's in these things because they're easier to find parts for...<P>It's because they don't want to have to wait until it snows to do donuts in the parking lot!<P>Everyone still has that part of them that's 3 years old and wants to color the pavement with their dual rear-mounted black crayons.  Yeah, they cost more than Crayola (tm) brand, but what do you expect?  It's a bigger canvas!  And a HELL of a lot more fun!<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
